"""Original STR design education: decision gates, examples and working records."""
# slug, title, lead, decision, execution, example, acceptance, fields, related owner route
CHAPTERS=[
('guest-and-property-brief','Define the guest and property brief',
'A rental design brief begins with activities and constraints. Guest count alone cannot tell a designer whether the property must support group meals, remote work, outdoor equipment, quiet sleep or several activities at once.',
'Write down the intended stay before selecting a style: arrival, luggage, cooking, dining, gathering, individual retreat and departure. Identify fixed property conditions such as stairs, bathroom distribution, exterior access and owner storage. Distinguish verified facts from preferences and assumptions. Research comparable listings for their advertised features and layout, while keeping the observation date and source. A blocked calendar is not proof of a booking, and an advertised nightly price is not realized revenue.',
'Convert the observations into room requirements. State the retained furniture, the maximum group you intend to accommodate, the practical dining plan and who will maintain every proposed amenity. For an operating rental, organize guest feedback by repeated functional issue instead of redesigning around a single comment. Give the designer a current floor plan, labeled wall photos and a list of decisions that require local verification. Keep legal occupancy and permitting questions with the appropriate local authority.',
'A hypothetical eight-person rental has comfortable beds but only four dining chairs. The brief does not immediately request a larger table: the host checks the dining room, circulation and patio use before choosing how meals will work. The result might be a revised indoor arrangement, a lower advertised capacity, or another practical plan supported by the site facts.',
'Approve the brief when every room has a stated function, known constraints and a person responsible for confirming open questions. Save the revision used to request proposals; later additions are scope changes.',
['Property identifier and brief revision','Intended guest activities','Room functions and capacity assumptions','Existing features and retained inventory','Observed competitor feature and dated source','Unresolved site question and verifier','Owner approval and date'],
'/resources/planning/'),
('scope-and-provider-selection','Choose the design and execution scope',
'Consultation, remote design, procurement and full-service installation transfer different tasks. Choose the scope by the work your local team can actually perform and the decisions you want to delegate.',
'Prepare one responsibility matrix before comparing designers. Include measurements, room planning, selections, ordering, delivery appointments, receiving, inspection, claims, storage, assembly, mounting, styling, cleaning and photography. Mark each task as included, excluded or unresolved for each proposal. A lower service fee may simply leave more coordination with the owner; a comprehensive package may still exclude specialized trades or a second site visit.',
'Use the researched company directory to build a relevant shortlist. Read the official source and confirm the chosen package rather than treating an entire company as one standardized product. Ask who signs off on dimensions, changes and completion. Request the deliverable format and revision allowance. For a remote project, identify the local receiver and installer before selecting a plan-only engagement. For a manager offering design, separate the design contract from recurring management terms.',
'A hypothetical owner compares a virtual design plan with a turnkey proposal. The local handyman can assemble furniture but cannot receive weekday freight. The owner adds a receiver and storage quote to the virtual option before comparing total scope. Either provider could still be the better fit, but the missing responsibility is now visible.',
'Proceed when the written agreement names responsibilities, exclusions, approvals and the completion process. Keep unresolved fees visible; do not silently enter zero for an unquoted task.',
['Provider and package name','Official source and proposal date','Deliverables and revision allowance','Owner and provider responsibilities','Local receiver and installer','Exclusions and unresolved costs','Approval and change procedure'],
'/alternatives/str-design-companies/'),
('landed-budget-and-reserves','Build the landed furnishing budget',
'A useful furnishing budget follows the complete path from approved item to usable room. Product prices alone omit several costs that can change the project decision.',
'Separate the budget into design and coordination, products, tax, freight, receiving, storage, assembly, installation, trade work, household essentials and final photography. Record the basis of each number: actual quote, allowance or unresolved estimate. Add a project reserve deliberately rather than hiding it inside every item. The appropriate reserve depends on uncertainty; this guide does not prescribe a universal percentage.',
'Build a room-level schedule and reconcile it to the property total. Keep quantity, unit price and total visible. Mark retained items separately so they are not purchased twice. Track deposits, balances, cancellations, credits and refunds alongside approved commitments. A paid deposit does not mean the complete project cost has been spent or that the item has arrived. Review cash timing before authorizing long-lead orders, custom goods or nonreturnable purchases.',
'In an illustrative budget, four dining chairs at $180 each total $720. A $95 delivery charge and $120 assembly charge bring the subtotal to $935 before any applicable tax or other charges. If one chair is canceled, revise the commitment and capacity plan; do not leave the original quantity in the installed inventory. These amounts are hypothetical arithmetic, not market price benchmarks.',
'Approve a purchase batch when the products, logistics, cash timing and reserve draw are visible. Reconcile the actual installed schedule to invoices at handover.',
['Room and item identifier','Quantity and approved unit cost','Tax, freight and receiving','Assembly and installation','Quote versus allowance status','Committed, paid and outstanding amount','Reserve decision and approver'],
'/tools/design-budget-calculator/'),
('measured-layout-and-delivery-access','Test layouts and delivery access',
'A room layout and a delivery route are two different fit tests. Both must work before an expensive furnishing order is approved.',
'Measure the room consistently, using a labeled sketch and the same units throughout. Record openings, door swings, fixed cabinetry, heating equipment, outlets and changes in ceiling or floor level. Photograph each wall and mark the measurement version. Tape proposed furniture footprints on the floor where feasible. Test circulation with drawers open and with convertible pieces in the configuration guests will actually use.',
'Trace the route from unloading to the final room: gates, parking restrictions, corridors, stairs, lifts, landings and turns. Compare packed dimensions as well as assembled dimensions. A removable leg does not prove that the remaining frame will fit a tight turn. Ask the carrier or qualified installer to review difficult access and identify who bears a failed-delivery charge. Recheck critical dimensions after flooring, trim or other construction changes.',
'A hypothetical sofa fits on the living-room plan but its packaged frame cannot pass a stair landing. Before ordering, the owner obtains the packed specifications and a delivery review. The selected replacement may have a different construction or delivery method; the decision follows verified access rather than the product photograph.',
'Approve the layout only after the actual room functions, critical dimensions and delivery route are checked. Refer building, egress and accessibility requirements to qualified local review.',
['Room and measurement date','Fixed features and opening directions','Furniture assembled and packed dimensions','Guest-use configurations tested','Unloading and access restrictions','Carrier access review','Critical uncertainty and verification owner'],
'/resources/layouts/'),
('concept-color-and-materials','Develop the concept and material palette',
'A coherent design concept provides a reason for the selections. It should support the property’s setting and guest activities while remaining practical to clean, repair and replace.',
'Create a restrained concept brief before collecting dozens of products. Identify the fixed finishes that will remain and the visual relationship between adjoining rooms. Decide which elements carry the concept and which provide a quiet background. Avoid using a trend as proof that guests will pay more. Design can be evaluated for coherence and usefulness without inventing an occupancy or nightly-rate uplift.',
'Test physical samples in the actual property, in daylight and with the installed evening lighting. Compare color, texture, reflections and visible wear. Obtain care instructions for the exact product and finish. Ask whether covers, spare parts or matching units can be purchased later. Keep the approved sample, SKU, finish and revision together. If a selected item goes out of stock, compare the substitute against those requirements instead of approving it on color alone.',
'A hypothetical host selects a pale upholstered dining chair from a screen image. Sample review shows that the care method is incompatible with the local team’s process. The host compares another fabric or chair model before placing the order, retaining the overall palette while changing the practical specification.',
'Approve the palette when the exact samples, care requirements and substitution rules are recorded. Keep product-specific technical requirements with the manufacturer instructions.',
['Concept statement and room role','Retained fixed finishes','Sample and exact product identifier','Daylight and evening observations','Care restrictions and cleaning owner','Replacement and spare-part options','Approved alternative criteria'],
'/resources/materials/'),
('sleep-bath-and-laundry','Plan sleep, bathing and laundry together',
'The bed, bathroom inventory and laundry process form one operating system. A visually finished bedroom can still be difficult to reset when its bedding, storage and washing requirements are unresolved.',
'Specify the actual bed frame, mattress and compatible bedding together. Confirm dimensions, assembly instructions, support requirements and access for changing linens. Keep charging, controls, luggage and everyday storage usable. Evaluate window treatments and room lighting from the guest’s sleeping position. Avoid assuming that a product label or a photograph proves comfort for every guest.',
'Build the textile inventory around the local turnover process. List beds, sizes, sets in use, spare sets and where clean and used items are stored. Check manufacturer washing and drying instructions against the equipment and laundry service available. Identify bottlenecks from real reset observations. For bathrooms, plan accessible everyday storage, towel placement, cleaning access and the household stock that the manager will replenish.',
'A hypothetical property mixes three mattress sizes and visually similar linen sets. The cleaner loses time finding the right replacements. The host labels storage by room and size, records the installed mattress dimensions and tests a complete reset. The problem is addressed through inventory and workflow rather than buying more unlabeled bedding.',
'Accept the setup after a complete practice reset confirms that the local team can find, clean, replace and store the actual inventory.',
['Room and installed bed dimensions','Frame and mattress instructions','Linen sizes and labeled storage','Care method and laundry capacity','Bathroom stock and replenishment owner','Reset observation and issue','Corrective action and retest'],
'/resources/comfort/'),
('kitchen-dining-and-gathering','Coordinate cooking, dining and gathering',
'A group rental needs a coherent activity plan beyond sleeping capacity. Test how guests will prepare food, eat, gather and move between those activities.',
'Inventory cookware, tableware, seating and appliances by the actual intended stay. Distinguish advertised amenities from optional conveniences. Check storage and counter use with the items present. A larger appliance is not automatically more useful if it consumes preparation space or cannot be maintained by the local team. Record manufacturer requirements and route technical installation questions to the relevant professional.',
'Test the arrangement as a guest would use it: open the dishwasher, pull out chairs, serve a meal and move between seating zones. Verify the dining table’s ordinary and extended configurations. Keep everyday items findable and use a room inventory so broken or missing pieces can be replaced consistently. In gathering spaces, compare comfortable seating with the group plan and preserve a route through the room.',
'A hypothetical six-person rental has six dining chairs, but two cannot be used when a cabinet door is open. The owner tests the real layout and adjusts placement before approving the room. The inventory records usable configurations rather than claiming capacity from chair count alone.',
'Accept the room when cooking, storage, dining and movement work together in the intended configuration and the cleaning team can reset the space consistently.',
['Guest activity and usable seating','Cookware and tableware inventory','Appliance model and instructions','Storage and counter layout','Door and drawer interference','Room reset photograph','Replacement stock and owner'],
'/resources/layouts/'),
('lighting-outdoors-and-amenities','Resolve lighting and outdoor amenity operations',
'Lighting and amenities need an operating plan as well as an attractive photograph. Establish who installs, maintains, checks and explains each feature before it appears in the listing.',
'Evaluate the arrival route, circulation, task lighting and evening room use. Check controls from the guest’s perspective and label confusing switches where appropriate. Compare glare and reflections in the actual furniture arrangement. Keep electrical work and product installation with qualified professionals and follow instructions for the exact product. Decorative choices do not establish code compliance.',
'For outdoor spaces, record exposure, drainage, seasonal storage and cleaning requirements. Compare materials using manufacturer guidance and the site’s conditions. Before adding a specialized amenity, obtain the necessary installation, permitting, insurance and maintenance review. Assign inspections, consumables, service calls and guest instructions. Estimate ongoing costs separately from the purchase and avoid promising a revenue return without property-specific evidence.',
'A hypothetical host orders outdoor cushions but has no dry storage for storm preparation. The operating brief adds a storage location and a person responsible for seasonal handling before the final selection. The owner also checks whether the care method is practical for the local team.',
'Approve each feature when installation, ongoing care, guest instructions and service ownership are documented. An amenity that cannot be maintained reliably is still an unresolved design decision.',
['Lighting zone and guest control','Exact product instructions','Outdoor exposure and storage','Install and permit review owner','Maintenance schedule and service contact','Guest-use instructions','Seasonal readiness check'],
'/resources/outdoors/'),
('procurement-receiving-and-claims','Manage procurement, receiving and substitutions',
'Procurement is a sequence of controlled decisions. Ordering the right product is only the beginning; receiving, inspecting and resolving exceptions determine what becomes usable inventory.',
'Keep one approved purchasing register with room, SKU, finish, quantity, quote, approval, order number and expected delivery. Confirm cancellation and return terms before purchase. Record the receiving contact and inspection process before the first shipment arrives. An unattended delivery can create a storage, weather or access problem even when the order itself is correct.',
'Inspect against the actual order and preserve claim evidence according to the seller’s procedures. Connect photographs to the shipment and product line. Separate a missing item from a damaged item and assign the next action. For substitutions, compare size, access, care, appearance, timing and the total landed cost. Require an approval before purchase and update every downstream record, including the floor plan and installed inventory.',
'A hypothetical delivery contains four chair cartons, but one has the wrong finish. The receiver photographs the label and chair, records three accepted units and one exception, and follows the seller’s remedy process. The purchasing tracker remains open until the corrected unit is received and inspected.',
'Close an order only after its accepted quantity, credits, replacement items and installation status reconcile. Keep open claims visible to the person deciding room readiness.',
['Approved item and quantity','Order and shipment references','Receiver and delivery appointment','Inspection evidence and exceptions','Claim deadline and case number','Substitution approval and documents','Accepted quantity and closure date'],
'/resources/launch/'),
('installation-photography-and-launch','Install, test and photograph the real property',
'Installation completion, photographic readiness and guest readiness are related but separate checks. Use evidence from the installed rooms before publishing the listing.',
'Compare delivered goods with the approved layout and inventory. Keep instructions and installation records for the exact products. Test doors, drawers, convertible furniture, lighting controls and storage in normal use. Send mounting, electrical, plumbing and other specialist concerns to the qualified person responsible. Record missing items and temporary arrangements explicitly.',
'Create a punch list with one issue per record, a room identifier, evidence, action owner and reinspection. Prioritize issues that prevent intended use. Before photography, complete the cleaning and styling reset and agree on a truthful shot list. Images should represent the amenities and layout guests will receive. Track selected image files and which channels use them so later changes trigger the necessary updates.',
'A hypothetical bedroom is styled while a temporary lamp substitutes for the approved fixture. The punch list records the substitution and the photographer is told whether to postpone that room. The owner does not publish a staged amenity that will be removed before the first guest arrives.',
'Accept launch readiness when the intended-use checks pass, essential issues are resolved and the photographed inventory matches the operating property. Preserve the final record and local handover.',
['Room and approved plan revision','Installed product and instructions','Functional test result','Punch-list owner and evidence','Photography readiness and exceptions','Selected image files and channels','Launch decision and signoff'],
'/resources/launch/'),
('tax-and-cost-segregation-records','Prepare the design records for tax and cost segregation review',
'Keep financial records while the project is happening. Reconstructing a bundled furnishing or renovation spend from scattered messages later can make the tax and property-study handoff harder.',
'Link each purchase to the property, room and installed item. Preserve invoices, payment records, freight, installation detail, refunds and substitutions. Keep acquired furnishings, later purchases and renovation work distinguishable in the record. Ask contractors for a useful description of the work and where it was performed. Do not assign tax classifications based on a designer’s label or assume a whole furnishing package receives one treatment.',
'Prepare a chronology showing acquisition, work, readiness for rental use and later changes, supported by the actual records. Keep removed and replaced components traceable where relevant. A cost segregation provider evaluates property components and evidence; the tax preparer evaluates the return treatment and taxpayer facts. Confirm engagement responsibilities before treating an estimate as an implemented result.',
'A hypothetical renovation invoice combines cabinets, appliances and painting. The owner requests a clearer vendor breakdown, retains the original invoice and adds room-level photos. The owner does not invent allocation percentages to fill the gaps. The study provider and preparer can then identify what further evidence is needed.',
'Complete the handoff when invoices reconcile to payments and inventory, open allocation questions are labeled, and the tax preparer has confirmed what additional information is needed. This chapter organizes records and does not determine deduction eligibility.',
['Property and item or work identifier','Invoice, payment and credit references','Acquisition versus later purchase','Room and supporting photos','Work and readiness chronology','Open allocation question','Study provider and return-preparer responsibilities'],
'/resources/'),
('post-launch-review-and-refresh','Review the operating property and plan refreshes',
'The design process continues after launch through observed guest use, cleaning feedback and replacement decisions. Use the operating record to decide what to change next.',
'Create a dated issue log covering repeated guest questions, furniture wear, difficult resets, maintenance calls and inventory gaps. Separate design observations from pricing, calendar availability and marketing changes. If booking performance changes, a simple before-and-after comparison does not establish that design caused it. Seasonality, market demand, price, availability and listing changes can also matter.',
'Prioritize function and operating reliability before decorative changes. Review whether a damaged component can be repaired, whether a replacement fits the existing layout and whether the exact product remains available. Compare complete costs, including downtime or additional visits where relevant. Keep the approved replacement linked to the old inventory and update care instructions, room reset photos and listing images.',
'A hypothetical host receives repeated messages asking where the bedside light switch is. The first response is a walkthrough of the actual room and controls, followed by a clear instruction or practical adjustment. Buying a new fixture is considered only after the observed issue is understood.',
'Approve a refresh when the issue, proposed remedy, actual cost and verification method are documented. Close it after a local check confirms the remedy works and the operating records reflect the installed result.',
['Issue, observation date and evidence','Guest or team impact','Design versus operating cause to review','Repair or replacement options','Approved landed cost','Updated inventory and images','Retest result and next review'],
'/resources/maintenance/'),
]
assert len(CHAPTERS)==12
